{
  "requirement": "If the accepted result cannot be deserialized into `R`, awaiting the builder\nreturns `ValidationError::Agent`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** If the accepted result can't be deserialized into `R`, awaiting the builder returns `ValidationError::Agent`.\n\n**Test.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[typed_submission_references_resolve_and_invalid_results_are_agent_errors]` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It scripts a `submit_result` call of `{\"result\":\"wrong\"}` and then awaits `ctx.ask::<bool>()`. It asserts `Err(ValidationError::Agent(_))`.\n\n**Why this exercises decoding.** I checked `tools.rs#fn[submitted_value]`. It only checks the envelope: exactly one key, `result`. It doesn't validate the value against the schema. So the string `\"wrong\"` is accepted by the harness and returned as the accepted result. The failure for `bool` then happens only at the `serde_json::from_value` step in `procedure.rs#impl[Ask<'a, 'd, D, R, P>]#2/fn[execute]`, which maps it to `ValidationError::Agent`. That is the behavior the requirement states. The assertion checks the error variant, which is what the requirement specifies.\n\n**Other cases in the same loop.** `{}`, `{\"other\":true}` and `{\"result\":true,\"extra\":true}` are envelope violations. They belong to `agent-harness.submission.invalid` and are not decoding failures. They add nothing here, but they do no harm.\n\n**Positive path.** The same test submits a valid `Answer` and decodes it successfully. That shows decoding is not simply failing on everything.\n\n**Gaps.** The requirement has a single obligation and a single failure condition, and both are tested and asserted. I found no gap.",
  "references": "e3a5fe45d43bd8249c3bba6c58efc85b15e9af5ed286059fe88369f67025424e",
